LANDING PR #416: gates that need no baseline. Pure core and view, no unsafe outside two crates, one-way crate layering, known sources (issue #336).

Branch story-336-no-baseline-gates, rebased onto main e6c34c82 (which now carries #341's coverage gate and its brokkr-seatbelt-probe crate). Its last commit admits that crate into the graph.

Changes:
- per-crate clippy.toml disallowed lists for core and view;
- crates/brokkr-cli/tests/layering.rs, whose scanner:
  - lexes comments and literals away and resolves use trees;
  - holds a closed std, core and alloc allowlist and closed pure macros, and refuses every builtin effect macro and any builtin-shadowing macro_rules;
  - requires exactly one lib at src/lib.rs plus test targets, and refuses build.rs, bins, examples, benches and relocated libs;
  - admits only two shapes under exactly #[cfg(test)]: a #[path] to a test path, and an alias of the crate itself;
- closed dependency sets for core and view, and an allowed graph over every workspace and external edge, dev edges included, read from cargo metadata, with [bans] parsed strictly;
- #![forbid(unsafe_code)] in five crates;
- cargo-deny check licenses bans sources, with licences still covering dev-only crates;
- view on BTreeMap and BTreeSet, a controller ruling;
- ARCHITECTURE.md cites the gates.

THREAT MODEL, as the operator ruled it on 2026-09-26: the purity gate must catch every realistic way code slips an effect into core or view BY MISTAKE, and fail closed on what it cannot read. Deliberate adversarial evasion is OUT OF SCOPE, and the gate documents this in tests/layering/main.rs and ARCHITECTURE.md. Rate an exotic, deliberate evasion as a LOW residual, not a security hold. Reserve high and security-hold for a realistic accidental route: an ordinary API, attribute or module shape a developer could write without meaning to evade anything, which the gate lets through, or a refusal that skips input instead of failing closed.

This is the sixth landing. The fifth (landing-pr-416-gates-that-need-n-b42ef00e) held on one legitimate HIGH: a macro-generated `mod $n;` compiled a file the scan never read. The latest commit refuses every `mod` inside a macro_rules! body, and the resolver refuses rather than skips any production mod it cannot follow. It also answers the five lows: normalised manifest lint keys, no --offline, one home per fact, no test clones, and a clean GIT environment. Before that, the fifth landing was The fourth (landing-pr-416-gates-that-need-n-ef8d124e) held on five routes. Commit 75f139b5 closes them:
- F1: attribute strings under path-taking keys are held to the allowlists, and unknown attribute strings are refused.
- F2: a per-dependency effect table, pinned to Cargo.lock versions, with drift failing.
- F3: rustc-style module resolution; a file is test code only when every declaration chain runs through exactly #[cfg(test)].
- F4: manifest lint overrides are refused.
- F5: repository Cargo config is refused.

The layering test is now a directory target, tests/layering/{main,scan}.rs. Judge whether these close their realistic classes.

The view BTreeMap move and the #341 interaction (brokkr-seatbelt-probe added by whichever PR merges second) are not findings.

This run lands one pull request under decision 0051 so the contribution gate can vouch for it. If a finding returns the branch, fix exactly that finding, prove any code or test change with a test bound by a compiling mutation, commit one signed-off commit ending "Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>", and never push. Do not run scripts/coverage-exact.sh; the controller runs it.
